  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 247 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: GOES-19 mid level water vapor imagery shows deep\ndry layer acrs our cwa, which has help to limit the areal \ncoverage of showers this aftn. A weak impulse is approaching the\nOttawa Valley with a pocket of better mid lvl moisture and \ninstability and should be near the SLV by 22z this evening. This\nwl quickly move eastward acrs the Dacks, before falling apart \nin the CPV by midnight tonight. Have highest pops over the \nSLV/Northern Dacks this evening. Additional s/w energy is \nlocated north of Lake Huron and wl swing acrs our cwa on Friday \nwith more scattered showers expected, especially favorable \nupslope areas of the Dacks and northern/central Greens. \nSoundings indicate lingering instability overnight, combined \nwith upslope flow under llvl caa, should keep clouds persisting \nin the mtns, even after sunset this evening. These clouds and \nwinds wl keep temps in the mid 30s to mid 40s. \n\nFor Friday, still thinking a few wet snow flakes are possible above \n3500 feet, especially as progged 850mb temps drop below 0C per \nlatest RAP13 data by 12z. Highest probability of precip wl be acrs \nthe northern/central Greens and into parts of the CPV on Friday \nmorning. Once again very steep lapse rates are anticipated on Friday \naftn with summit temps in the mid/upper 30s, while valleys warm into \nthe upper 50s to lower 60s. Favorable mixing should help to advect \ndrier air aloft toward the sfc with dwpts falling back into the 30s \nand lower 40s again on Friday aftn with breezy west/northwest winds \n15 to 30 mph. \n\nBy 12z Saturday 1032mb high pres is progged to be centered over \nnorthern NY, but  925mb to 850mb winds are still 15 to 30 knots from \nthe northwest and some 850 to 500mb moisture is already approaching \nthe area associated with waa. Have bumped temps upwards slightly \nfrom my thoughts yesterday, but still thinking mid/upper 20s SLK to \nupper 30s/lower 40s near Lake Champlain. Plenty of sunshine and \ncomfortable temps are expected on Saturday with highs mid 50s to \nlower 60s. As sfc high pres shifts toward the Gulf of Maine, \nsoutherly gradient develops on Sat night into Sunday with moderate \nwaa. Progged 925mb temps warm btwn 10-12C by 12z Sunday with \nincreasing 925mb to 850mb winds of 20 to 35 knots. Progged soundings \nindicate a sharp and shallow llvl inversion developing Sat night, \nsupport a wide range temps from cooler valleys to warmer \nmidslopes/summits with highs back into the 60s on Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The forecast is quite challenging late Sunday into \nMonday as the exact track of Hurricane Isaias will determine how wet \nthe end of the weekend and early next week. The global deterministic \nguidance continues to show the remnants of Isaias bringing rainfall \nthe region Sunday night and Monday morning while the ensemble \nguidance remains a bit split. A strong Bermuda high pressure system \nover the western Atlantic is expected to suppress how far east the \nremnants can initially track. A strengthening mid-level jet is \nexpected to develop on the southern side of the remnants as the \nhurricane becomes extratropical which should push any precipitation \nassociated with the remnants quickly across the region with no \nsignificant impact with rainfall amounts likely less than half of an \ninch. An upper level trough will likely continue in the wake of the \nremnants with a building ridge of high pressure expected across the \ncentral US. This will put Vermont and northern New York under \nnorthwesterly flow aloft with the potential for one or two cold \nfronts through the middle of next week with chances for rainfall \nassociated with each of these fronts. Again, no significant \nrainfall is expected. The pattern overall is wet and messy, but \nisn't expected to be impactful.",
